  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (20 minutes)</title>
<para>In the Numbers Screen activity, students will explore an arithmetic function using the terms “input” and “output.” As students build a function, the equation representation updates dynamically and includes a simplified representation. Students can observe the resulting outputs as in the previous activity, and as an explicit table of ordered pairs. </para>

<title>Launch</title>
<list type="enumerated">
  <item>Circulate as students work on part 3. If some groups seem behind, offer assistance if they have questions. </item>
  <item>After 5 minutes or so, give students a time update that they should be on to #4.  </item>
  <item>When groups have finished, bring the class together for a whole-class discussion. Have four different students share their responses to #6.</item>
  
</list>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<div class="os-raise-familysupport">
  <para><link url="https://phet.colorado.edu/sims/html/function-builder/latest/function-builder_en.html">Access the interactive simulator</link> to begin this project.</para>
</div>
<para>For questions 1-3, use the following instructions. </para>

<blockquote>Use the simulation screen labeled “Numbers”. Note you can switch between simulation screens using the toolbar located below the function builder.</blockquote>


<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
<para/><item>Build a function. Fill in the function builder and table. Access the input and output information by clicking the gray tabs above and below the function builder.</item> 
<image height="400" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/2effc7c16d2e7d366079e2c971e7ca01afb6d372"/><para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>


<para/><item> What is the output when the input is 10? Switch papers
  with a teammate and check that you found the correct
  output. 💬</item>
  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>

  <para/><item>
      Describe how to find the output for your function if given
      any input. <emphasis> Challenge yourself to describe in multiple ways. </emphasis>
    </item>
  
  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<para>For questions 4-8, use the following instructions.</para>
<blockquote>Use the simulation screen labeled “Equations”. Note you can switch between simulation screens using the toolbar located below the function builder.</blockquote>
<para>
  </para><item>
    Build a custom function. Fill in the function builder and representations. Access the table and graph information by clicking the gray tabs above and below the function builder.
    <image height="400" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b252c80348b84c3a925f4ed0aeb179aec3bc68d7"/></item>


<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>

<para/><item>What is y when x is 100?</item>
  
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>

<para/><item>
    Manipulate your function in different ways. Describe the effects on the table, graph, and equation that each of your actions has.
</item>
  <table class="os-raise-wideequaltable">
    <thead>
      <row>
        <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Action</entry>
        <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Effect on table</entry>
        <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Effect on graph</entry>
        <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Effect on equation</entry>
      </row>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <row>
        <entry>Click the up arrow on the addition operation <image height="50" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/e0a877d1de82ef133f366ee3025d8c563e4e7f66"/></entry>
        <entry> </entry>
        <entry> </entry>
        <entry> </entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry/>
        <entry> </entry>
        <entry> </entry>
        <entry> </entry>
      </row>      
      <row>
        <entry/>
        <entry> </entry>
        <entry> </entry>
        <entry> </entry>
      </row>      
      <row>
        <entry/>
        <entry> </entry>
        <entry> </entry>
        <entry> </entry>
      </row>
    </tbody>
  </table>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>

  <table class="os-raise-wideequaltable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Action</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Effect on table</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Effect on graph</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Effect on equation</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>Click the up arrow on the addition operation <image height="50" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/e0a877d1de82ef133f366ee3025d8c563e4e7f66"/></entry>
      <entry>When I clicked up, the \(y\) values increased by 1 for each click.  </entry>
      <entry>The graph moved up the number of spaces that I had clicked. </entry>
      <entry>The number at the end of the equation changed (constant term).</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>Click the down arrow on the addition operation <image height="50" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/e0a877d1de82ef133f366ee3025d8c563e4e7f66"/></entry>
      <entry>If I clicked down, it decreased 1 unit for each click. </entry>
      <entry>The graph moved down the number of spaces that I had clicked.</entry>
      <entry>The number at the end of the equation changed (constant term).</entry>
    </row>      
    <row>
      <entry>Click on the up or down arrows on the multiplication operation <image height="50" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/040d740d29548a03b2ea17a0572458038ef67646"/></entry>
      <entry>The numbers in the table got farther apart when I went up or down. At one point they were 2 units apart then after I clicked it, they were 3 units apart. Something weird happened when the multiplier was 0. At zero, everything just equaled 1 because the zero got rid of everything else.</entry>
      <entry>This changed the slope or tilt of the line. When I clicked up, the direction changed to where the line tilted up as I moved to the right. When I clicked down, the line got steeper when it slanted down as I moved to the right. </entry>
      <entry>Both numbers in the equation changed - the one at the end (constant term) and the one in front of the x (coefficient).</entry>
    </row>      
    <row>
      <entry>Click on the up or
        down arrows on the
        subtraction operation <image height="50" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b2dc2fb014d98e80b6eab494c60bbe22921e552c"/></entry>
      <entry>When I click down, all the \(y\)-values move down 1 unit, too. </entry>
      <entry>The graph slid down the \(y\)-axis by 1 unit each click. The slope or tilt of the line did not change. </entry>
      <entry>In the equation, the value inside the parentheses changed with each click. In the simplified equation, this changed the last number (constant term). </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>


<para/><item>What does your graph look like? What other graphs can you make? </item>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>

<para/><item>Briefly describe how different operations impact the graph of your function.</item>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Addition
    <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
  </item>
  
  
  <item>Subtraction
    <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
  </item>
  
  
  <item>Multiplication
    <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
  </item>
  
  
  <item>Division
    <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
